{"gid":"2026_01_CLE_JAX","week":1,"away":"CLE","home":"JAX","away_col":"#FF7A45","home_col":"#19D3C5","spread":7.5,"total":40.5,"fav":"JAX","hpts":24,"apts":16,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 13 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Script leans JAX.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Chris Rodriguez Jr.</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile CLE throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>JAX</strong> host the <strong>CLE</strong> as favorites of <strong>7.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>40.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>JAX 24\u201316</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>39% of its snaps with JAX in front</strong>, <strong>35% within one score</strong>, and <strong>26% with JAX chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>JAX should control</strong>. A favorite of 8 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>JAX</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>CLE</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>CLE</strong> to run more total plays than JAX \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for CLE's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong> \u2014 JAX are stingy against the run (109 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Shedeur Sanders</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 JAX allow <strong>252 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 JAX concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58). A rec-yards spot.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>KC Concepcion</strong> (WR2) \u2014 JAX leak 44 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 JAX are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CLE throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Low-total grind</strong> \u2014 a 40 total signals a slower, run-leaning game; skew toward unders, especially on secondary pass-catchers.</p><h3>JAX \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>JAX are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Chris Rodriguez Jr.</strong> (11 carries, 50 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though JAX's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Parker Washington</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 63 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brenton Strange</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 36 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>CLE \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>CLE are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 CLE project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong>'s rushing projection (13 carries, 48 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, CLE funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>7 targets for 58 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 55 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 8-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"CLE","col":"#FF7A45","players":[{"name":"Deshaun Watson","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":64.86,"book":177.5,"over_th":51.14,"under_th":73.52,"over_am":"\u2212115","under_a